   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend chapter 95 of the laws of 2000 amending the state finance law, the general municipal law, the public buildings law and other laws relating to bonds, notes and revenues, in relation to extending the effectiveness thereof",
    "basePrintNo": "S4865",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S4865-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §27, Chap 95 of 2000",
    "lawSection": "State Finance",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S4865\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend chapter 95 of the laws of 2000 amending the state\nfinance law, the general municipal law, the public buildings law and\nother laws relating to bonds, notes and revenues, in relation to\nextending the effectiveness thereof\n\nPurpose:\n\nTo extend through June 30, 2020 the authority of the Commissioner of\nGeneral Services to enter into leases for real property for a term not\nexceeding 15 years to meet the space requirements of State\ndepartments, agencies and offices.\n\nSummary of Provisions:\n\nThis bill would extend by five years the authority of the Commissioner\nof General Services to enter into 15-year leases on behalf of State\ndepartments, agencies and offices.\n\nExisting Law:\n\nPublic Buildings Law § 3(12) gives the Commissioner of General\nServices the authority to lease real property for a term not to exceed\n10 years, except that the Commissioner may enter into a 15-year lease\nwhere the Commissioner finds that a longer term is in the best\ninterests of the State. The Commissioner's exceptional authority for\n15-year leases is set to expire on June 30, 2015.\n\nPrior Legislative History:\n\nPublic Buildings Law § 3(12) was amended by L. 2000 c. 95 § 27 to give\nthe Commissioner the authority to enter into 15-year leases, but such\nauthority was set to expire on June 30, 2005. The five-year sunset\nprovision has twice been extended for additional five-year periods by\nL. 2005 c. 56 pt. J and by L. 2010 c. 141.\n\nStatement in Support:\n\nIn order to effectively and economically provide for the space\nrequirements of State departments, agencies and offices under varying\noperational and economic circumstances, it is beneficial for the State\nto be able to use different terms for real property leases. The\nflexibility offered through the ability to enter into leases for terms\nnot exceeding 15 years has resulted in reduced costs to State agencies\nand overall savings to the State. On average the cost difference\nbetween a 10- and 15-year term can offer savings of 7% to 10%.\nContinuing this authority will permit the State to enter into leases\nwhere it is necessary to provide a longer period of time to amortize\ncosts of necessary tenant improvements undertaken by the landlord.\n\nBudget Implications:\n\nThis bill will continue the cost savings that 15-year leases have\ngenerated since 2000.\n\n\nLocal impact:\n\nNone.\n\nEffective Date:\n\nThis bill would take effect immediately.\n\n",
